<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Role in the World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How the Canal Impacts Panama's Future</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Evaluate spoken information from radio or television. Interactive: Engage in role-play scenarios to resolve everyday conflicts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend and evaluate information from a news broadcast on a local issue (e.g., "After evaluating the news broadcast, it's clear that the proposed improvements to the Panama Canal's infrastructure are designed to ease congestion and enhance global trade efficiency.")
            Can role-play resolving a disagreement over environmental concerns vs. economic growth (e.g., "What would you say if a classmate suggested prioritizing development over sustainability?")
            Can use questions to confirm understanding of key ideas in media broadcasts (e.g., "Why did the speaker mention increased funding for infrastructure improvements around the Panama Canal?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing a large map of Panama highlighting the Canal, and projecting images of large container ships navigating the locks, along with pictures depicting "congestion" at ports and "environmental groups" protesting. Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what they see and what comes to mind regarding Panama's global influence and challenges. The teacher plays a short (15-second) audio clip of bustling port sounds (ships, horns, cargo loading) and asks, "What do these sounds suggest about the Panama Canal's 'operation'?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words from the provided list, such as "infrastructure," "efficiency," "global trade," and "environmental concerns" by writing them on the board and modeling their pronunciation, particularly focusing on the long vowel sound /oʊ/ in "global" and "local" and the reduced vowels in "environmental." The teacher then uses example sentences: "The Canal's 'infrastructure' is constantly being improved to enhance 'efficiency'." and "Many 'environmental groups' raise 'concerns' about the Canal's 'impact' on local 'ecosystems'." Students listen and repeat. The teacher explains the meaning of words like "congestion" using synonyms or simple definitions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a port has a lot of 'congestion,' does it mean ships move quickly or slowly?" (Expected answer: Slowly.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something has 'environmental concerns,' is it usually good or bad for nature?" (Expected answer: Bad for nature.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> "Panama's Role in the World".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher introduces a short (1.5-2 minute) audio news broadcast titled "Panama Canal's Future: Balancing Growth and Sustainability." The broadcast includes discussions on new "investments" in "infrastructure," efforts to improve "efficiency" and "prevent" "delays," and the ongoing debate with "environmental groups" regarding the Canal's "impact" on "ecosystems." It naturally incorporates passive voice ("The Canal has been managed by the Panama Canal Authority since 1999," "new routes are being explored") and present perfect continuous ("The Canal has been impacting global trade, but it has also been causing environmental concerns").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic-style audio news broadcast script, recorded clearly (genre: news report, register: formal/informative, format: audio file).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Evaluate spoken information from radio or television.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Listening - Gist):</b> Students listen to the broadcast once for the main idea.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the main topic of the news broadcast? (A) A celebration of the Canal's history. (B) Current challenges and future plans for the Panama Canal. (C) A report on global warming.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which two key aspects of the Canal's future are discussed? (A) Tourism and entertainment. (B) Economic growth and environmental concerns. (C) Sports and education.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying key vocabulary (e.g., "congestion," "infrastructure," "investments," "environmental groups," "efficiency"), grammar (passive voice, present perfect continuous) and practicing pronunciation of /oʊ/ and multisyllabic words from the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening for Specific Vocabulary):</b> Students receive a handout with 8-10 sentences from the news broadcast, each containing a blank where a key vocabulary word should be. They listen to the broadcast again (second time) and fill in the blanks. Example: "New _______ are being made to further enhance the Canal's _______." (investments, efficiency) "The Canal has been _______ global trade, but it has also been causing _______ concerns." (impacting, environmental). The teacher pauses after each sentence or phrase for students to write.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Listening for Grammar and Pronunciation):</b> Students listen to specific segments of the broadcast (teacher selects 3-4 key sentences). First, they identify if the sentence uses passive voice or present perfect continuous. Second, they focus on pronunciation: The teacher plays sentences containing words like "global," "local," "monitor," "operation," and "environmental," and asks students to listen carefully for the /oʊ/ sound and the reduced vowels. Students then repeat these words chorally and individually, trying to match the intonation and pronunciation from the audio.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Clarifying Questions):</b> Students work in pairs to compare their answers for the vocabulary fill-in-the-blanks. They discuss any discrepancies and, if needed, ask the teacher to replay specific sections of the audio. They are encouraged to use questions to confirm understanding of key ideas in the broadcast, e.g., "Why did the speaker mention increased funding for infrastructure improvements around the Panama Canal?" or "Can you explain what 'congestion' means in the context of shipping 'routes'?" This also activates their sociolinguistic competence by discussing respectfully.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Role-play scenario based on the news broadcast.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student takes the role of a representative from the "Panama Canal Authority" or "government" advocating for further "investments" in "infrastructure" to enhance "efficiency" and "global trade." The other student takes the role of a representative from an "environmental group" raising "concerns" about the ecological "impact" of such developments on "ecosystems." Students must listen carefully to each other's points, respond, and try to find common ground or express hypothetical ideas using second/third conditionals based on what they heard in the news broadcast and their own opinions (e.g., "If we don't improve the 'infrastructure,' global trade could face significant 'delays'," or "If the 'environmental impact' had been properly 'monitored' from the start, we wouldn't be facing these 'concerns' today.") They practice discussing the canal's impact respectfully, using trade-related terms appropriately. The teacher circulates, providing support and noting language use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students continue their role-play from Stage 4. The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' ability to listen, comprehend, and evaluate spoken information, engage in interactive role-play scenarios, and use the target vocabulary and grammar structures (passive voice, conditionals, present perfect continuous) effectively and respectfully in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., 'global,' 'environmental,' 'impactful').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words from the lesson (e.g., 'infrastructure,' 'efficiency,' 'congestion,' 'environmental groups,' 'impact,' 'investments').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas when responding to partner's points (listening comprehension).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask at least two clarifying questions based on listening to partner's input (e.g., "Could you elaborate on the 'long-term' 'impact' of those 'investments' on local communities?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates respectful communication regarding Panama's role and trade.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase related to the Panama Canal's future that you learned to listen for today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you think you can now evaluate information from a news broadcast about local issues after listening to today's audio?" (Scale of 1-5, 5 being very well).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening to the news broadcast and identifying specific details or new vocabulary, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the vocabulary and grammar (like passive voice or conditionals) we practiced today help you understand more complex spoken information in English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this listening lesson? The students seemed engaged with the authentic news broadcast and the role-play allowed for good interactive listening. The explicit pronunciation practice for /oʊ/ and reduced vowels was well-received.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for next time? Some students struggled to identify specific passive voice structures solely through listening; perhaps a short pre-listening grammar warm-up for this structure would be beneficial. Ensuring the audio clarity for all words, especially multisyllabic ones, is crucial.</p>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read an article or report that expands on the same theme, "How the Canal Impacts Panama's Future," using many of the vocabulary words and grammatical structures introduced and practiced today through listening. This will reinforce their understanding and prepare them for deeper engagement with the content.</p>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>